WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.420
If calcium entry needs glutamate and depolarization together, what happens when

2
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.080
several inputs reach one cell?

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.080
Picture one CA1 cell receiving separate input pathways.

4
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.900
You can stimulate each one on its own.

5
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.380
Three properties were measured before the gate was known.

6
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:23.380
Can the gate explain them?

7
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:27.123
First, an input that stays silent during the train.

8
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:29.083
Does it change?

9
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:36.420
No. It released no glutamate, so its NMDA receptors let in no calcium, however

10
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.040
depolarized the cell became.

11
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.340
This mechanism does not potentiate it.

12
00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:43.803
That is input specificity.

13
00:00:44.823 --> 00:00:49.003
You saw it in the first recording, where the untrained control pathway stayed flat.

14
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:52.317
Next, a train to just a few fibres.

15
00:00:53.257 --> 00:00:54.757
It can fail to induce LTP.

16
00:00:55.797 --> 00:00:57.497
The same train to many fibres succeeds.

17
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.200
The balance point shows why.

18
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.060
A few synapses barely move the local voltage, so the gate barely moves.

19
00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:11.023
Many synapses together pull the balance point up, close to where calcium entry is

20
00:01:11.023 --> 00:01:12.023
greatest.

21
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:13.897
That is cooperativity.

22
00:01:14.757 --> 00:01:17.437
These voltages come from a single patch of membrane.

23
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:22.117
Real dendrites differ, so read the direction, not the exact values.

24
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:26.047
Now take two pathways, one weak and one strong.

25
00:01:26.807 --> 00:01:28.907
The strong one can potentiate on its own.

26
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.510
Stimulated alone, the weak pathway fails.

27
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:37.450
Its spines get glutamate, but too little depolarization to clear the block.

28
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.810
Now stimulate the weak pathway during the strong pathway's train.

29
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.510
A third pathway gets no stimulation at all.

30
00:01:46.383 --> 00:01:48.863
Predict each pathway's slope an hour later.

31
00:01:54.883 --> 00:01:57.643
The strong pathway is potentiated, as before.

32
00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:00.837
The weak pathway is potentiated too.

33
00:02:01.717 --> 00:02:03.017
It supplied its own glutamate.

34
00:02:03.897 --> 00:02:07.317
The strong input supplied the depolarization that cleared the block.

35
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.970
The third pathway stays near baseline, because it released no glutamate.

36
00:02:14.083 --> 00:02:17.003
That is associativity, a Hebbian rule in a dish.

37
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:22.557
A spike fired by the cell and spreading back into the dendrite can play the strong

38
00:02:22.557 --> 00:02:23.557
input's part.

39
00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:26.597
This is the shape an association needs.

40
00:02:27.457 --> 00:02:31.697
A weak input, like a cue, gains strength when active with a strong one, like an

41
00:02:31.697 --> 00:02:32.697
outcome.

42
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:37.857
The takeaway: one gate, reading two signals, explains input specificity,

43
00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.317
cooperativity and associativity.

44
00:02:41.117 --> 00:02:44.197
That holds when the inputs depolarize the right spines enough.
